<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Suetonius</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 69–after 122</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century">2nd century</a> · Also known as Gaius Suetonius Tranquillus</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Suetonius was a Roman biographer and imperial official who served as a secretary to the emperors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trajan">Trajan</a> and Hadrian. His Lives of the Twelve Caesars, covering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domitian">Domitian</a>, is a main source for the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and records details such as Vespasian's campaigns in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>. Many of his other writings, on grammarians, poets and other subjects, survive only in part.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/suetonius.jpg" alt="Suetonius" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Achille Réveil · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Suetonius_etching_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Suetonius</b> records that Vespasian took away the freedom of Byzantium, along with that of Achaea, Lycia, Rhodes and Samos, and around 73 CE it was incorporated into a Roman province.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Cunobelinus, ruling from about 9 to 40 CE from Camulodunum, which he took from the Trinovantes, extended influence over Kent and much of the south-east; <b>Suetonius</b> called him king of the Britons.</blockquote><a href="/state/catuvellauni">Catuvellauni</a></li><li><blockquote>In 43 and 44 the future emperor Vespasian, leading the legio II Augusta, took more than twenty strongholds in the south-west according to <b>Suetonius</b>; a Roman fort was built inside Hod Hill.</blockquote><a href="/state/durotriges">Durotriges</a></li></ul></section>
